**Diary — 2026-03-18 — Porting the CUDA engine to HIP on gfx906**

Today I moved the core CUDA engine onto ROCm/HIP targeting `gfx906` (Vega 10 / MI50 class). The goal was not a full rewrite, just a clean port of the hot path: kernel launches, memory management, streams/events, and the reduction kernels.

### 1. Build setup

I started with a minimal HIP translation unit:

```bash
hipcc -O3 -std=c++17 -x hip -mcpu=gfx906 engine.hip -o engine
```

For a mixed host/device project, I split the CUDA-specific code into `.hip` files and kept host-only code in `.cpp`.

The first thing I did was replace CUDA runtime calls:

```cpp
// CUDA
cudaMalloc(&d_x, n * sizeof(float));
cudaMemcpy(d_x, h_x, n * sizeof(float), cudaMemcpyHostToDevice);
cudaMemset(d_y, 0, n * sizeof(float));
cudaFree(d_x);

// HIP
hipMalloc(&d_x, n * sizeof(float));
hipMemcpy(d_x, h_x, n * sizeof(float), hipMemcpyHostToDevice);
hipMemset(d_y, 0, n * sizeof(float));
hipFree(d_x);
```

I also replaced CUDA streams and events:

```cpp
hipStream_t stream;
hipEvent_t start, stop;

hipStreamCreate(&stream);
hipEventCreate(&start);
hipEventCreate(&stop);

hipEventRecord(start, stream);
kernel<<<grid, block, smem, stream>>>(...);
hipEventRecord(stop, stream);
hipEventSynchronize(stop);

float ms = 0.0f;
hipEventElapsedTime(&ms, start, stop);
```

