**Diary — 2026-03-14 — Porting the CUDA engine to HIP on gfx906**

Today I moved the core CUDA engine over to HIP on an AMD MI50 / Vega 20-class GPU, target ISA `gfx906`. The goal was not a toy demo: I wanted the existing tile-based compute engine to run under ROCm with the same numerical output and a sane performance profile.

### What I changed first

The CUDA engine had three main GPU-facing pieces:

1. A kernel launcher wrapper around `cudaLaunchKernel`
2. Device memory management using `cudaMalloc`, `cudaMemcpy`, `cudaFree`
3. Stream/event synchronization using `cudaStream_t` and `cudaEvent_t`

I replaced the CUDA runtime calls with HIP equivalents:

```cpp
#include <hip/hip_runtime.h>

#define CHECK_HIP(x) do { \
    hipError_t err = (x); \
    if (err != hipSuccess) { \
        fprintf(stderr, "HIP error %s:%d: %s\n", __FILE__, __LINE__, hipGetErrorString(err)); \
        exit(1); \
    } \
} while (0)
```

Memory allocation became:

```cpp
CHECK_HIP(hipMalloc(&d_tile, bytes));
CHECK_HIP(hipMemcpy(d_tile, h_tile, bytes, hipMemcpyHostToDevice));
CHECK_HIP(hipFree(d_tile));
```

Streams and events:

```cpp
hipStream_t stream;
hipEvent_t start, stop;

CHECK_HIP(hipStreamCreate(&stream));
CHECK_HIP(hipEventCreate(&start));
CHECK_HIP(hipEventCreate(&stop));

CHECK_HIP(hipEventRecord(start, stream));
engine_kernel<<<grid, block, smem_bytes, stream>>>(...);
CHECK_HIP(hipEventRecord(stop, stream));
CHECK_HIP(hipEventSynchronize(stop));

float ms = 0.0f;
CHECK_HIP(hipEventElapsedTime(&ms, start, stop));
```

